{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: replace proposal #16914 with a target-aware landing-obligation proposal that preserves its audit findings while resolving Planner dissent #16915. Do not implement code; return one replacement proposal suitable for ratification and retire #16914 in its favor.","body":{"conditions":"1. Reuse the existing work-group rule that every related activity has one current target branch. For new implementation work, make the destination explicit and durable: repository identity, full target ref, and exact measured target head. Main is only a legacy default, never the conceptual target.\n2. Define terminal evidence, not a merge mode. Distinguish: (a) landed — the approved candidate is incorporated by a sealed receipt into the named target ref, whether that ref is main, a release branch, or an integration branch that will later become a PR; (b) accepted external handoff — Gitseq publishes and the requester accepts an exact base-to-head handoff while another system owns the target landing; and (c) reported resolution — no Git landing or handoff was part of the requested outcome. Do not classify an ordinary non-main branch landing as external handoff.\n3. Make a hold orthogonal to the terminal obligation. A held landing remains owed to its named target. Show awaiting-authorization waiting on the hold owner, then awaiting-landing waiting on the performer after an exact release. The authorization must bind request, candidate, approval, target ref, and target pre-head. An ordinary completion report cannot discharge that landing obligation.\n4. Bind sealed receipts to the target repository/ref as well as target pre-head and resulting head. Today gs merge acts on a checkout but the durable receipt does not name the ref, so a target-relative approved-not-landed join cannot be proved from the log. Define movement, deletion, rename, remote publication, and legacy-receipt behavior.\n5. Make all landing visibility target-relative: approved_not_landed means not incorporated in the request's named target, never merely absent from main. Project the target, hold owner, release state, approval, candidate, and terminal evidence through status, work, inspect, worktrees, CLI, MCP, Table, and Graph. Preserve one-completion-per-promise and the carried-or-abandoned rule for approved-head supersession.\n6. Reconcile this design with the reviewed but changes-requested external-PR handoff work from #15750/#15756 rather than creating a competing lifecycle. Answer its six review blockers, including authorship, profile-version and retroactivity, non-merge authority, every status enumeration, completion precedence, and ref verification at acceptance.\n7. Specify compatibility and migration for existing requests, approvals, artifacts, reports, receipts, and unmerged worktrees. Keep the audit recovery order, but do not begin recovery implementation under this design request.\n8. The replacement proposal must be concise enough to ratify as scope, explicitly list later design-note and implementation requests, and authorize no code itself.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}